            <div class="imagemeta col-md-5 col-md-offset-2">
                <h3>Summary</h3>

                <p>I worked with Google on AI implementation for the Android app used by its developers and partners for bug reporting.</p> 
              <p>Our work was able to <strong>significantly reduce waste</strong> on a long, tedious process and <strong>free up a truly unbelievable amount of developer time.</strong> </p>
              <p>The project contract doesn't allow me to share details, but here’re some extremely nonspecific points about my contributions and some of the questions that came up (and would come up in any implementation of AI on an internal tool.)</p>

            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-3">
                <img class="image" src="/images/projects/google/bugs_3up.jpg" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
      
            <div class="images col-md-3 col-md-offset-1">
                <img class="image shadow" src="/images/projects/google/bugs_overflowing.jpg" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-4">
        
                <h3>Design Challenge</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Use AI to speed up a task that people are doing (in some cases) <strong>many times every day.</strong></li> 
                    <li>Streamline a process requiring a <strong>large number of user inputs</strong> which take a long time to fill out and are prone to errors.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reduce the number of duplicate reports</strong> while still ensuring that new information is captured.</li>
                </ul>
            
            </div>

            
            <div class="imagemeta col-md-4 ">

                <h3>Design Questions</h3>
                <ul>
                <li>When is it appropriate for AI to interrupt a user-driven process? What conditions make it <strong>empowering vs. annoying</strong>?</li>
                <li><strong>What kind of UI creates an appropriate cognitive volume</strong> for those interruptions to make users welcome them and not immediately look for a way to turn them off?</li>
                <li>What does a satisfying AI-assist look like for users with <strong>deep expertise and entrenched habits with the task at hand?</strong> How does it differ from the way we offer assistance to someone doing something for the first time?</li>
                </ul>

            </div>
            
        </div>   
    </div>
</section>


